// The console lock: one lock for the terminal's transmit path (GitHub issue
// #663, option A).
//
// It orders every writer of the transmit queue, the terminal echo queue, the
// column and wire-state words, and the FIFO drain, so that the order bytes
// reach the wire is the order the lock was taken -- on whichever CPU. It is
// what Linux's tty write lock, FreeBSD's per-tty mutex and OpenBSD's spltty
// section are: one queue, one lock, contention accepted. Before it, core 0
// was the only writer of all of this, with interrupts masked and no lock,
// and a process on another CPU had to hand its bytes to a ring that core 0
// drained at moments of its own (kernel/models/ConsoleTx.tla shows the
// reordering, and the lock order below).
//
// LOCK ORDER. run -> console, and the console lock is innermost: it is taken
// while the process-run lock is held (whole output chunks are admitted under
// the run guard, and the receive handler echoes under it), and nothing is
// taken while it is held. In particular the transmit interrupt lets go of it
// BEFORE it takes the run lock for the wake; keeping it across the wake is
// the NESTED variant of the model, which deadlocks against a writer that
// holds the run lock and wants this one.
//
// NOTHING UNDER IT LOGS. A critical section that called kernel_boot_log would
// take this lock again on the way to the UART and hang, because a Mutex has
// no owner and no recursion count. The functions that run under it carry the
// guard as a parameter, so a caller cannot reach them without holding it, and
// they are written without `logs`.
//
// Current limitations (2026-09-30):
// - THE KERNEL LOG AND THE TERMINAL ARE STILL TWO STREAMS. A peer's kernel
//   log lines go through the per-CPU publication ring and core 0 moves them
//   here at its own moments, as before; only TERMINAL output is ordered by
//   this lock. That split is deliberate (kernel/CONCURRENCY.md, "Reporters
//   must not take locks": an oops or a BREAK must not wait for a lock), and
//   it is Linux's own split between printk and tty output.
// - DDB AND THE CRASH CONSOLE DO NOT TAKE IT. They stand the queue down and
//   write through uart_debug_putc. The world stop that precedes them waits for
//   every other CPU to take an interrupt, which a CPU inside this lock does
//   not until it leaves; the critical sections are short and bounded by the
//   wire, so that is a delay, not a hang, but a holder stuck for good would
//   stop the debugger entering.
use "kernel/lib/mutex.tkb";

let mut console_tx_lock: Mutex;

// `[lock: addr]` names the one lock a guard came from, and linear, so no path
// through a critical section can leave it held.
linear struct ConsoleGuard[lock: addr] {
    private flags: usize;
}

// mutex_acquire masks interrupts, then takes the word, and returns the state
// a matching release restores -- so this is safe from the transmit and
// receive interrupt handlers and from code that already has them masked.
fn console_lock() -> ConsoleGuard[console_tx_lock]
        !{acquires_lock_50_console, lock_guard_50_console,
          irq_masking_guard} {
    let mut guard: ConsoleGuard[console_tx_lock] =
        { mutex_acquire(&console_tx_lock) };
    return guard;
}

fn console_unlock(guard: sink ConsoleGuard[console_tx_lock]) {
    mutex_release(&console_tx_lock, guard.flags);
}
